name: permissions and identity
boss: Gatekeeper
flavor: Every door has a lock. I hold the modes.
brief: Every file has three sets of permissions -- owner, group, everyone --
brief: and three bits in each: read, write, execute. ls -l shows them as
brief: rwxr-xr-x. chmod changes them.
brief:
brief: Numeric modes are the fast way once they click: r=4, w=2, x=1, added up
brief: per set. 755 is rwxr-xr-x, 644 is rw-r--r--, 600 is rw------- .
brief:
brief: On xos everything runs as root and the root filesystem is read-only, so
brief: these matter for what you write to tmpfs and to disks you mount -- not
brief: for defending this machine from you.
---
setup: you just wrote @script@ and ./@script@ says permission denied
q: make @script@ executable with chmod
a: chmod +x @script@
state: mode:@script@:755
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: +x adds execute for everyone at once, owner group and other
---
q: {anyone can run @script@, but only you can edit it|make @script@ runnable by all, writable only by you|open @script@ to run and read for all, write stays yours}, with chmod
a: chmod 755 @script@
state: mode:@script@:755
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: 7=rwx, 5=r-x -- add read(4)+write(2)+execute(1) per set
---
q: {you can edit @txt@, everyone else can only read it|give yourself read+write on @txt@, read-only to others|put @txt@ back to default: you write, others read}, with chmod
a: chmod 644 @txt@
state: mode:@txt@:644
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: 6=rw-, 4=r-- -- you get read+write, everyone else read-only
---
setup: @txt@ holds something you don't want anyone else touching
q: {lock @txt@ so only you can read or write it, no one else|shut everyone but you out of @txt@ entirely|@txt@ is yours alone -- no access for group or other}, with chmod
a: chmod 600 @txt@
state: mode:@txt@:600
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: 6=rw-, 0=--- -- nothing at all for group or other
---
q: {@script@ shouldn't run anymore -- strip execute entirely|@script@ isn't ready to run -- take execute off for everyone|stop anyone, yourself included, from running @script@ as-is}, with chmod
a: chmod -x @script@
state: mode:@script@:644
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: a bare -x with no letter in front strips execute from all three sets
---
q: {your group needs to edit @txt@, leave owner/other alone|let the group write to @txt@, nothing else touched|give the group write access on @txt@, nothing else changed}, with chmod
a: chmod g+w @txt@
state: mode:@txt@:664
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: g targets the group set specifically, leaving owner and other untouched
---
q: {owner and group keep @txt@ access, lock everyone else out|strip @txt@'s permissions for everyone outside owner/group|zero out what everyone else can do to @txt@}, with chmod
a: chmod o= @txt@
state: mode:@txt@:640
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: = sets the listed permissions exactly, and an empty list means none
---
q: make everything under @dir@ readable with chmod, subdirs included
a: chmod -R a+r @dir@
why: primitive-cost
ref: chmod
teach: chmod -R
hint: -R walks into every subdirectory and applies the change there too
---
q: {who are you|ask the shell who you are logged in as|print your current username}
a: whoami
a: id -un
out: ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_-]*$
ref: whoami
teach: whoami
hint: whoami is a shortcut for id -un
---
q: %show% your uid, gid and every group you're in, all at once
a: id
out: uid=
ref: id
teach: id
hint: id with no flags prints uid, gid and every group at once
---
q: a command wants your numeric uid, nothing else -- %show% it with id
a: id -u
why: primitive-cost
ref: id
teach: id -u
hint: -u limits output to just the numeric uid
---
q: %show% your username with id, instead of the raw uid number
a: id -un
out: ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_-]*$
ref: id
teach: id -n
hint: -n added to -u prints the name instead of the number
---
q: %show% just your numeric group id, nothing else
a: id -g
why: primitive-cost
ref: id
teach: id -g
hint: -g limits output to just the numeric gid
---
q: %show% the default permission mask new files are created with
a: umask
out: ^[0-7]{3,4}$
ref: umask
teach: umask
hint: umask alone prints the current mask without changing it
---
q: {make every new file private to you by default from now on|new files keep coming out readable by others -- fix the default|stop new files defaulting to readable by anyone but you}, with umask
a: umask 077
why: primitive-cost
ref: umask
teach: umask
hint: umask is subtracted from the default; 077 leaves only owner permissions
---
q: %show% everything about @txt@ -- size, owner, mode, timestamps
a: stat @txt@
out: Size
ref: stat
teach: stat
hint: stat alone prints every field it knows about a file
---
setup: a script needs @txt@'s size as a plain number, nothing else
q: %show% just the size of @txt@ in bytes with stat, nothing else
a: stat -c %s @txt@
out: ^[0-9]+$
cmp: ref
ref: stat
teach: stat -c
hint: -c takes a format; %s is size, %n name, %a permission bits
---
q: you want @script@'s mode as a plain octal number, with stat not ls
a: stat -c %a @script@
cmp: ref
ref: stat
teach: stat -c
hint: %a in the format string is permission bits
---
q: forget @txt@ itself -- with stat, %show% the filesystem it lives on
a: stat -f @txt@
out: ID|Block
ref: stat
teach: stat -f
hint: -f switches from the file's info to the filesystem it lives on
---
q: {@txt@ is a symlink -- stat what it points to, not the link|@txt@ is a symlink -- follow it and stat the real file underneath|don't stat the symlink @txt@ itself, stat what it points at}
a: stat -L @txt@
out: Size
ref: stat
teach: stat -L
hint: -L follows the link and reports on its target instead of the link itself
---
q: {back-date @txt@'s timestamp to 2024-03-01, not right now|@txt@ needs to look last touched on 2024-03-01|stamp @txt@ with 2024-03-01, not the current time}, with touch
a: touch -d 2024-03-01 @txt@
why: primitive-cost
ref: touch
teach: touch -d
hint: -d takes an explicit date/time instead of using now
---
q: {@txt2@ should carry the timestamp @txt@ already has|copy @txt@'s timestamp onto @txt2@, skip the date|make @txt2@'s timestamp match @txt@'s}, with touch
a: touch -r @txt@ @txt2@
why: primitive-cost
ref: touch
teach: touch -r
hint: -r copies another file's timestamp instead of specifying one yourself
---
setup: you want to check what an unprivileged account would see, not root
q: {run id as the user nobody instead of as root|check what id looks like as nobody, not root|see your identity as nobody would see it, not as root}
a: setuidgid nobody id
why: root
ref: setuidgid
teach: setuidgid
hint: setuidgid USER PROG switches to USER's uid and gid, then runs PROG -- learn runs your answers this way
---
q: make @txt@ immutable with chattr
a: chattr +i @txt@
why: root
ref: chattr
teach: chattr
hint: +i makes it unchangeable even by root; chattr -i clears it again
---
q: set @txt@ append-only with chattr
a: chattr +a @txt@
why: root
ref: chattr
teach: chattr
hint: +a is the append-only attribute, useful for a tamper-resistant log
---
q: %list% @txt@'s ext2 file attributes with lsattr
a: lsattr @txt@
why: root
ref: lsattr
teach: lsattr
hint: lsattr prints the attribute letters chattr would set, in one fixed column
